# {{.Title}}

A web application built with **Gleam** and **Howdy**, created by `howdy new`.
It serves a themed home page built from Howdy UI components, static files
from `priv/public`, and comes with hot reload for development and a test
suite that sends requests through the app.
{{- if .Full}}

Under `gleam dev` the Howdy admin shows {{if .Database}}the database and {{end}}a trace
of every request, and a console runs Gleam inside the server.
{{- end}}

## Requirements

- Gleam 1.18+ and Erlang/OTP{{- if eq .Source "git"}}
- Git to download Howdy, Howdy UI and the development-only {{if .Full}}hot reload,
  admin and console packages{{else}}hot reload
  package{{end}} from {{if .Repository}}`{{.Repository}}`{{else}}GitHub{{end}} at the Howdy {{.Release}} release, tag
  `v{{.Release}}`, as configured in `gleam.toml`.
{{- end}}

## Start the dev server

```sh
gleam deps download
gleam dev
```

Open **http://127.0.0.1:8787**{{if .Full}} for the site and
**http://127.0.0.1:8787/_howdy** for the admin{{end}}. Changes under `src/` rebuild and refresh
open pages; changes under `priv/public/` refresh them too. Compile errors
show in the browser while the previous code keeps serving. Restart after
editing `dev/`, `gleam.toml` or `.env`.

{{- if .Full}}

### The admin

**http://127.0.0.1:8787/_howdy** is the Howdy admin, mounted by
`dev/{{.Name}}_dev.gleam`:

- **Telemetry** shows every request as a timeline of its spans and the log
  lines it wrote, and points out failures.

The admin has no login, so it only exists under `gleam dev`, answers only on
localhost, and never ships: it is a dev dependency, and `gleam run` and
`gleam export erlang-shipment` leave `dev/` out.

### The console

The console is [GSH](https://hexdocs.pm/gsh/), a Gleam REPL, running inside
the dev server, so what you type uses the same processes as the site.
Shortcuts live in `src/console.gleam`, which every session imports.

Start the dev server inside a console, in one terminal:

```sh
gleam run -m gsh -- {{.Name}}_dev
```

The site, hot reload and the admin all run as usual, with a prompt:

```text
gsh(1)> console.get("/")
```

To attach to a dev server that is already running, start it as a named
Erlang node, then attach from a second terminal:

```sh
# Terminal 1
ERL_EPMD_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1 \
ERL_FLAGS="-sname {{.Name}}@localhost -setcookie dev -kernel inet_dist_use_interface {127,0,0,1}" \
gleam dev

# Terminal 2
ERL_FLAGS="-kernel inet_dist_use_interface {127,0,0,1}" \
gleam run -m gsh -- --remsh {{.Name}}@localhost --cookie dev
```

GSH says `Connected to remote node`. The flags keep the node on 127.0.0.1:
anyone who can connect to it can run any code in your app, so never expose
it beyond your machine. GSH compiles each line in `src/`, so open pages
refresh after every command.

### Without hot reload
{{- end}}

`gleam run` starts the server without the reload watcher.

## Configuration

Configuration is read from the environment, with `.env` in the working
directory loaded first. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` to change it.

| Variable | Default | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `PORT` | `8787` | Listening port |
| `HOST` | `127.0.0.1` | Address to listen on; `0.0.0.0` in a container |
| `DOT_ENV_PATH` | `.env` | Which file to load, set in the shell |
{{- if .Full}}
| `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` | unset | `gleam run` sends traces to this OpenTelemetry collector, such as `http://localhost:4318` |
{{- end}}

## Test

```sh
gleam test
```

Tests use `howdy/testing` to send requests straight to the app, without a
socket. See `test/{{.Name}}/features/home/home_controller_test.gleam`.

## Layout

```text
src/
├── {{.Name}}.gleam           # composition root: controllers, middleware, listener
{{- if .Full}}
├── console.gleam             # console shortcuts
{{- end}}
├── {{.Name}}/
│   ├── features/
│   │   └── home/
│   │       ├── home_controller.gleam  # routes for the home page
│   │       └── pages/
│   │           ├── index.gleam            # the home page
│   │           └── not_found_page.gleam   # the 404 page
│   └── layouts/
│       └── default_layout.gleam   # what every page is rendered inside
└── tasks/
    └── css.gleam            # writes priv/public/assets/ui.css
dev/
└── {{.Name}}_dev.gleam      # `gleam dev` entry point with hot reload{{if .Full}}, admin and telemetry{{end}}
priv/public/
└── assets/
    ├── style.css            # your styles
    └── ui.css               # generated Howdy UI styles (commit it)
test/
{{- if .Docker}}
bin/start                      # starts the app in the image: run{{if .Database}}, migrate{{end}} or remote
Dockerfile                     # builds the image to deploy
{{- end}}
```

Group new features by folder under `src/{{.Name}}/features/`, each with its
own controller, and register the controller in `src/{{.Name}}.gleam`. The
other folders in `src/{{.Name}}/` belong to Howdy's generators, so a feature
can have any name without clashing with them.

## Layouts

Everything around a page's content, from the HTML document with the theme
and stylesheets to the header, comes from a layout in
`src/{{.Name}}/layouts/`. The home page uses `default_layout.gleam`:

```gleam
default_layout.render(ctx, "About", [ui.h1("About"), ui.p([text("...")])])
```

Call `render_live` instead for a page with live sections (`live.mount`): it
also loads the script they connect with, which other pages leave out.

Pages that `howdy create page`, `live_page` and `pages` write use the
default layout, or another one with `--layout <name>`, and
`howdy create layout <name>` makes a new one to change.

## The 404 page

An address the site does not have shows `src/{{.Name}}/features/home/pages/not_found_page.gleam`
to people, rendered with the default layout, and change it as you like.
API clients, `fetch` and curl get Howdy's JSON error instead: the middleware in
`src/{{.Name}}/platform/not_found.gleam` shows the page only when the request's
`Accept` header prefers HTML, as a browser's does, and leaves any 404 page a
controller rendered itself alone.

## Components

Pieces of view that pages reuse live in `src/{{.Name}}/components/`, or in
a controller's own `components/` folder when only its pages use them.
`howdy create component [controller] <name>` writes one with a `view()` to
add to a page's content. `howdy create live_component [controller] <name>`
writes a Lustre server component and routes its WebSocket; put it on a page
with its `mount()`, in a page that renders with `render_live`.

## Email

`howdy add mail` adds Howdy Mail. The app's services in
`src/{{.Name}}.gleam` get a mailer, so controllers send with
`services.mailer`: through the provider the environment names under
`gleam run`, and into an outbox under `gleam dev`{{if .Full}}, which the admin shows
under Mail with a preview of every email{{end}}. `howdy create email <name>` adds
another email.

## Styles

During `gleam dev` the Howdy UI stylesheet is generated on request. For
`gleam run` and deployment it is read from disk, so regenerate it after
changing themes or adding copied components:

```sh
gleam run -m tasks/css
```

## Deploy
{{- if .Docker}}

The `Dockerfile` builds an image that runs the app:

```sh
docker build -t {{.Name}} .
{{- if eq .Database "sqlite"}}
docker run --rm -p 8787:8787 -v {{.Name}}_data:/data {{.Name}}
{{- else if eq .Database "postgres"}}
docker run --rm -p 8787:8787 -e DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/{{.Name}} {{.Name}}
{{- else}}
docker run --rm -p 8787:8787 {{.Name}}
{{- end}}
```
{{- if eq .Database "postgres"}}

`docker compose --profile app up --build` runs it beside the local
PostgreSQL, to try it before deploying.
{{- end}}

The image compiles the app with `gleam export erlang-shipment`, which
leaves out `dev/` and the dev dependencies, and runs it as an unprivileged
user on `0.0.0.0:8787`. Once the app has
[islands](https://tryhowdy.dev/docs/ui/islands), it runs
`gleam run -m howdy/ui export` instead, which bundles them for the browser
first.
{{- if eq .Database "sqlite"}} The SQLite database is kept in the `/data`
volume, so it outlives the container. Only one container at a time can use
it, so run a single replica.
{{- end}} It uses the Gleam and Erlang versions the project was made
with; change them at the top of the `Dockerfile` when you upgrade.
`bin/start` starts the app in the image:

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `bin/start run` | Serve the app; what the image runs |
{{- if .Database}}
| `bin/start migrate` | Apply pending migrations, then exit |
{{- end}}
| `bin/start remote` | Open an Erlang shell inside the running app |

Set `HOWDY_NODE_NAME` and `HOWDY_COOKIE` to start the app as a named
Erlang node, for `bin/start remote` or to join other nodes in a cluster.
`bin/start` explains them. Distribution is off without them, and nothing
can attach to the app.

Turning it on opens a door into the app: anyone who can reach the node
and knows the cookie can run any code in it, using its database and
reading its secrets with no sign-in. So:

- Make the cookie long and random, such as `openssl rand -hex 32`, and
  keep it in the platform's secrets, never in the repository or the image.
- Never publish the distribution ports. Erlang's port mapper listens on
  4369 and the node on `HOWDY_DIST_PORT`, or on any port without it; keep
  both on a private network that only the app's own nodes can reach, and
  never `-p` them from Docker or open them in a firewall.
- Distribution traffic is not encrypted, so only run it across a network
  you trust.
{{- end}}

Whatever runs the app, it is ready for production:

- **Health checks.** `GET /up` answers `{"status":"ok"}` while the app
  runs{{if .Docker}}, and the image's `HEALTHCHECK` asks it{{end}}. It is answered
  ahead of the routes and middleware and is not logged, so point a load
  balancer or platform at it.
- **Clean stops.** On SIGTERM the server stops taking connections and
  gives requests in flight 8 seconds to finish, inside the 10 seconds
  `docker stop` waits. Change it with `howdy.shutdown_timeout` in
  `configured`.
{{- if .Database}}
- **Migrations.** The app applies pending migrations as it starts, and a
  release that fails to migrate never starts serving. Copies of the app
  that start together take turns. To migrate as a step of its own before
  the new version starts, run {{if .Docker}}`bin/start migrate`{{else}}`gleam run -m tasks/migrate`{{end}} and set
  `MIGRATE_ON_START=false`.
{{- end}}
- **Configuration** comes from the environment. Set it on the platform
  rather than shipping `.env`{{if .Docker}}, which `.dockerignore` leaves out of the image{{end}}.

## Upgrading Howdy

{{if eq .Source "hex" -}}
This project uses Howdy {{.Release}}: every Howdy package in `gleam.toml`
comes from Hex, from its version in that release up to its next major
version. `gleam update` moves them to the newest versions within those
ranges, and `manifest.toml` records the ones in use. To move to a new major
release, raise the ranges of every Howdy package together, run `gleam deps
download`, and check the release's notes for anything you need to change.
{{- else -}}
This project uses Howdy {{.Release}}: every Howdy package in `gleam.toml`
comes from the release tag `v{{.Release}}`, so they always match. To move
to a newer release, change `ref = "v{{.Release}}"` to the new tag on every
one of them, run `gleam deps download`, and check the release's notes for
anything you need to change.
{{- end}}
